HLPCLO {
[1;36mThe [1;37mCLOAK[1;36m command will turn on or off the cloaking system, if your ship is
equipped. The cloaking system consumes large amounts of power and you should be
careful not to drain all your energy pool. The cloaking system takes a few
seconds to charge once the command is issued. Issue the command as follows:

   [1;37mcloak [1;32m[[1;37mon[1;32m/[1;37moff[1;32m][1;37m
} T GE help cloak message

HLPCLS {
[1;36mThe [1;37mCLS[1;36m command simply clears the screen.
[1;37m} T GE help cls message

HLPDEC {
[1;36mThe [1;37mDECOY[1;36m command deploys a single decoy. Decoys proceed away from your ship
and send a sensor signature that resembles your ship. If a torpedo or missile
comes near the decoy, the decoy will explode and destroy the inbound weapon.
The decoy will self-destruct after a period of time to prevent stray decoys
floating around in your navigation path. You can deploy up to ten decoys at a
time, and the more that are deployed, the greater the odds are of a successful
interception.
[1;37m} T GE help decoy message

HLPDES {
[1;36mThe [1;37mDESTRUCT[1;36m command will begin a self-destruct countdown timer that, when it
reaches zero, will destroy your ship. The countdown can be aborted with the
[1;37mABORT[1;36m command.

The neutron shock wave from your ship's explosion may damage ships in the
vicinity. If your ship's shields are raised when the self-destruct initiates,
the shock wave will be contained.
[1;37m} T GE help destruct message

HLPFLU {
[1;36mTopic: Neutron flux

All ships operate by the power provided by the neutron flux drive. The neutron
flux is recharged by loading in a new flux pod. Until recently, this was
occasionally a challenging process, as many ship systems would sometimes
completely drain the flux before a pod could be replaced. All ships now sold at
Zygor will now automatically replace flux pods as needed; it is no longer
necessary to manually switch pods.

If you wish, you can enter [1;37mFLUX[1;36m to replace your current flux pod. This command
presumes that you want it changed now, and does not warn you if there is still
a good percentage of power left in the old pod. The old pod is jettisoned. Your
power is restored to full charge automatically.

Ships are also capable of recharging the flux pile by absorbing the naturally
occuring background ion radiation of the galaxy, at a rate of 2 units every
three seconds. This means a ship can still travel at impulse even if a ship has
run out of flux pods.

It is your responsibility to make sure that you keep your ship stocked with
flux pods, because if you run out, it will be a long trip to the nearest base
at impulse.
[1;37m} T GE help flux message

HLPHEL {
[1;36mThe [1;37mHELP[1;36m command will display a vast amount of information on Galactic Empire.
Type [1;37mhelp[1;36m for a list of subjects, and [1;37mhelp [1;32m[subject][1;36m for information on that
subject.
[1;37m} T GE help help message

HLPHYP {
[1;36mIn hyperspace, a ship's standard phasers are useless. The hyper-phaser,
however, can be deadly. The hyper-phaser beam is very narrow but very powerful
and can destroy another ship with only a few well placed shots. Unlike the
standard phaser, you can't adjust the width of the beam. When in hyperspace,
issue the [1;37mPHASER[1;36m command as follows:

[1;37mphaser [1;32m[direction]

  [1;32mdirection[1;36m = the heading at which to fire the phasers, usually the bearing
              of another ship given in the "scan sh" command. With the
              hyper-phaser, you must be pinpoint accurate to score a hit.

Examples: [1;37mphaser 4       [1;36mfire 4 degrees off starboard
          [1;37mpha 180        [1;36mfire directly behind you
[1;37m} T GE help hyper message

HLPIMP {
[1;36mThe [1;37mIMPULSE[1;36m command directs your impulse (sub-light) engines and sets your
course and speed. Issue the impulse command as follows:

  [1;37mimpulse [1;32m[speed] [degrees/[1;37m@[1;32mheading]

  [1;32mspeed [1;36m= the impulse speed between 0 and 99 (99 is .99 light speed)
  [1;32mdegrees [1;36m= degrees to rotate to port (negative) or starboard (positive)
  [1;32mheading [1;36m= the absolute direction regardless of your current heading

Examples: [1;37mimpulse 5     [1;36malter the speed to warp 0.05, not changing the heading
          [1;37mimpulse 10 5  [1;36malter the speed to warp 0.10 and turn the ship
                        5 degrees to the starboard (right)
          [1;37mimp 20 -25    [1;36malter the speed to warp 0.20 and turn the ship
                        25 degrees to the port (left)
          [1;37mimp 50 @140   [1;36malter the speed to warp 0.50 and turn the ship
                        140 degrees absolute
[1;37m} T GE help impulse message

HLPJAM {
[1;36mThe [1;37mJAMMER[1;36m command will launch a jammer device from your ship. It disrupts all
scanner systems on all ships within range of the device, yours included. It is
useful for covering an escape or distracting an opponent. The closer you are to
the jammer, the more severe the jamming effect is, so don't assume someone
can't see you just because you can't see them.
[1;37m} T GE help jam message

HLPLOC {
[1;36mThe [1;37mLOCK[1;36m command locks your fire control systems onto a ship. As long as the
ship is within scanner range, the fire control systems will remain locked onto
that specific ship.

Commands like [1;37mMISSILE[1;36m, [1;37mTORPEDO[1;36m, and [1;37mSCAN[1;36m can then be used with [1;37m@[1;36m instead of the
ship letter. When you are locked onto a ship, its name will appear highlighted
in scans and reports.

When you lock onto a ship, that ship's scanners will detect the lock and report
that information to the ship's commander, however once the lock is engaged,
that ship will not be able to detect how often you are scanning it.

Cybertrons are known to consider setting a scanner lock to be a hostile act.

Issue the command as follows:

  [1;37mlock [1;32m[ship letter]

[1;36mOnce a ship is destroyed the lock is released automatically. Use the [1;37mLOCK[1;36m
command with no options to release the lock.
[1;37m} T GE help lock message

HLPMAI {
[1;36mUse the [1;37mMAINTENANCE[1;36m command when you are in orbit at Zygor or any other
sufficiently populated planet. The maintenance crews will start repairing your
ship and charge you a nominal fee of [0;33m2500[1;36m at Zygor and [0;33m200[1;36m anywhere else.

Leaving orbit early will cease all repairs on the ship. If the planet has a
trade password set, it will be required.

Maintenance crews will not operate on any ship that is determined to be engaged
in hostilities, as defined by the Galactic Codes of Engagement.

Cloaked ships will not be serviced unless the ship owner and planet owner are
the same.
[1;37m} T GE help maintenance message

HLPMIN {
[1;36mThe [1;37mMINE[1;36m command causes a neutron mine to be deposited at your current
location. You specify how long until detonation (between 1 and 50 ticks, each
tick being about six seconds), permitting you time to leave the spot for
safety. Mines are useful when you are being tailed by another ship. Be aware
that they are very powerful and you can also be damaged by them if you don't
get far enough away. Issue the command as follows:

  [1;37mmine [1;32m[time]

  time [1;36m= the time until detonation in ticks (approx 6 seconds)
[1;37m} T GE help mine message

HLPMIS {
[1;36mThe [1;37mMISSILE[1;36m command fires a hyper-missile at a given ship with a given charge.
This missile guidance system will attempt to lock on the ship prior to firing
the missile. If a lock is made, the missile is launched.

Missiles must be charged from your neutron flux pile, and will drain your
ship's flux pile by the amount of the charge. A missile's charge can be between
2000 and 20000; if you don't specify the default of 5000 is used.

Issue the command as follows:

  [1;37mmissile [1;32m[ship letter/[1;37m@[1;32m] [energy]

  ship letter [1;36m= the ship you are firing upon (or [1;37m@[1;36m for the locked on ship)
  [1;32menergy [1;36m= the energy from your neutron flux pile to charge into the missile.

Examples: [1;37mmissile A 2000   [1;36mfires a missile at ship A which cause minor (if any)
                   damage, and drains your ship's flux by 2000
          [1;37mmissile C 20000  [1;36mfires a missile at ship C which will cause major
                   damage, and drains your ship's flux by 20000
          [1;37mmis @            [1;36mfires on the ship that a previous [1;37mLOCK[1;36m command has
                   locked onto, and drains your ship's flux by 5000

Type [1;37mHELP BATTLE[1;36m for more information on missiles.
[1;37m} T GE help missile message

HLPNAV {
[1;36mThe [1;37mNAVIGATE[1;36m command will display the bearing and distance to the center of the
sector you specify. If you do not specify a sector, a default of [1;34m0 0[1;36m will be
used. Issue the command as follows:

  [1;37mnavigate [1;32m[sector]
[1;37m} T GE help navigate message

HLPNEW {
[1;36mThe [1;37mNEW[1;36m command is used to purchase a new ship, a new phaser or shield
system for your current ship, or a ship upgrade at Zygor.

Issue the command as follows:

  [1;37mnew [1;32m[[1;37mshield[1;32m/[1;37mship[1;32m/[1;37mphaser[1;32m/[1;37mupgrade[1;32m] [type]

  [1;32mtype [1;36m= the type of shield, phaser, ship, or upgrade you wish to purchase.

You must be orbiting around Zygor in Sector 0 0 to issue this command. A
complete list of ship classes and prices can be obtained from the [1;37mHELP CLASS[1;36m
command. For phaser and shield prices, use the [1;37mHELP NEWPRICE[1;36m command.
[1;37m} T GE help new message

